# ONE LINE ENDING IN THE REPOSITORY, and it is LF.
#
# Without this, what gets committed depends on which tool last wrote the file.
# Several files in this repo drifted to CRLF that way, and the cost is not
# cosmetic: a file whose endings differ from the branch you are merging has
# EVERY line marked as changed on both sides, so git cannot line the two change
# sets up and reports the whole file as one conflict — even when the edits are in
# completely different places.
#
# That is exactly what happened merging master into the parking branch:
# `Tile.vue` and `game.ts` came out wholly conflicted, and with the endings
# normalised the two sets of edits turned out to have ZERO overlapping regions.
# Twelve changed regions on one side, fifteen on the other, none of them touching.
# An hour of hand-resolution for a merge git should have done by itself.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` fixes what it stores and
# checks out. Working copies on Windows get LF too, which every tool in this
# project (node, vite, vitest, eslint, prettier) is happy with.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never touches the bytes.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
